                "text": "**Analyzing Grid Arrangement**\n\nI've been examining the spatial relationships between the input and output grids. Specifically, I've observed that the output is constructed from a 2x2 arrangement of the input's 3x3 blocks, which contain 4 elements of value 2. The separation between these blocks involves single rows and columns filled with zeros. I'm focusing on the precise arrangement of these blocks within the 7x7 output grid.\n\n\n**Mapping Block Placements**\n\nI'm now concretely mapping the 3x3 input blocks to their positions in the 7x7 output.  I've found specific examples where blocks are positioned Top-Left, Top-Right, Bottom-Left, and Bottom-Right based on coordinates within the original data. Now, I have to verify these findings against the data of the second train.\n\n\n**Investigating Block Equivalencies**\n\nI'm now diving into the specifics of block placement. I noticed in the second training set, the output blocks match specific input blocks. Block B from train 2 becomes the top-left, and Block A becomes the top-right! Now, I'm checking if the solid blocks from the input, which are absent in train 2, influence block placement elsewhere.\n\n\n**Confirming Block Identity**\n\nI've just confirmed that the block in the bottom-right of train 2's output is, in fact, Block D from the input, containing the values 0,0,2; 2,2,0; and 2,2,2. This realization helps me to understand how the mapping works! Now, I am exploring the arrangement of the other blocks.\n\n\n**Verifying Block Positioning**\n\nI'm now verifying my hypothesis about block placement by testing it against the first training set. I've analyzed the specific block locations and confirmed that their positions are determined by a left/right and top/bottom arrangement. I'm confident in how the top-left, top-right, bottom-left, and bottom-right blocks are mapped. I also made sure that the input positions line up with the output!\n\n\n**Determining Block Locations**\n\nI'm now 